window.GRAMMAR = {"lessons": [{"id": "nominal", "title": "Sentences with no “is”", "sub": "البيت كبير — “the house (is) big”", "body": ["Palestinian Arabic has no word for “is / am / are” in the present. You just put the two things side by side: <b>البيت كبير</b> — literally “the house big” — means “the house is big.” Same with “I’m tired” (<b>أنا تعبان</b>) or “the coffee’s hot” (<b>القهوة سخنة</b>).", "This is one of the first things that makes you sound natural: don’t hunt for a verb where English has one. Subject, then the describing word, and you’re done."], "examples": [{"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["قريبة"]}, {"ar": "المسا بالبيت أحلى وقت.", "en": "The evening at home is the nicest time.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "المي زرقا والرملة دافية.", "en": "The water is blue and the sand is warm.", "src": "story-beg-24", "title": "The Sea", "hi": ["زرقا", "دافية"]}, {"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["حلو"]}]}, {"id": "article", "title": "The definite article الـ", "sub": "“the”, glued to the front", "body": ["There’s one word for “the”: <b>الـ</b> (al-), stuck onto the front of the noun — <b>البيت</b> “the house”, <b>الولد</b> “the boy”.", "When the noun starts with a “sun letter” (ت ث د ذ ر ز س ش ص ض ط ظ ل ن), the <b>ل</b> of الـ assimilates: <b>الشمس</b> is written al-shams but <i>said</i> <b>ish-shams</b>, <b>الرمل</b> is <b>ir-raml</b>. With the other “moon letters”, you hear the l normally: <b>القمر</b> <b>il-2amar</b> (the ق is a glottal stop in city speech). You don’t have to memorise the list — your ear picks it up fast."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["الصبح"]}, {"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["المدرسة"]}, {"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["العربي"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["الطاولة"]}]}, {"id": "pronouns", "title": "The people: I, you, he, she…", "sub": "subject pronouns", "body": ["The subject pronouns. Note that Palestinian keeps a separate “you” for men and women, and the verb usually already tells you who’s acting — so these often get dropped for emphasis only."], "tables": [{"title": "Subject pronouns", "rows": [["أنا", "ana", "I"], ["إنت", "inta", "you (m)"], ["إنتي", "inti", "you (f)"], ["هو", "huwwe", "he"], ["هي", "hiyye", "she"], ["إحنا", "i7na", "we"], ["إنتو", "intu", "you (pl)"], ["هم", "humme", "they"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["هو"]}, {"ar": "أنا بحب الزيت والزعتر كتير.", "en": "I really like oil and za'tar.", "src": "story-beg-05", "title": "Breakfast", "hi": ["أنا"]}, {"ar": "أنا اسمي سامي وعندي عيلة كبيرة.", "en": "My name is Sami and I have a big family.", "src": "story-beg-02", "title": "My Family", "hi": ["أنا"]}, {"ar": "إحنا الولاد، كنا نساعد ونذوق سرا.", "en": "We kids used to help and taste secretly.", "src": "story-int-23", "title": "The Night of Eid", "hi": ["إحنا"]}]}, {"id": "bpresent", "title": "Everyday actions — the b- present", "sub": "بحب، بشرب، بروح", "body": ["For what you do regularly or are doing now, spoken Palestinian puts a little <b>بـ</b> (b-) on the front of the present verb: <b>بحب</b> “I like”, <b>بشرب</b> “I drink”, <b>بروح</b> “I go”. This is the everyday workhorse tense — most sentences you say will use it.", "The prefix shifts a bit by person — <b>بحب</b> (I), <b>بتحب</b> (you), <b>بيحب</b> (he), <b>بنحب</b> (we). The <b>Verbs</b> section conjugates all of these for any verb."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بروح"]}, {"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بحب"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["بنقعد"]}, {"ar": "بعد الغدا، بننام شوي.", "en": "After lunch, we nap a little.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["بننام"]}]}, {"id": "progressive", "title": "Right now — عم", "sub": "عم بشتغل — “I’m working (right now)”", "body": ["To stress that something is happening <i>right this moment</i>, put <b>عم</b> (3am) before the verb: <b>عم بشتغل</b> “I’m working”, <b>عم باكل</b> “I’m eating”. Without عم, the plain b- present covers both “I work” and “I’m working”; عم just makes the “right now” explicit."], "examples": [{"ar": "طار العصفور بالسما ونحنا عم نتفرج.", "en": "The bird flew into the sky while we watched.", "src": "story-int-28", "title": "The Bird", "hi": ["عم", "نتفرج"]}, {"ar": "مرة بالسوق، شفت ولد صغير عم يبكي لحاله.", "en": "Once at the market, I saw a little boy crying by himself.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["عم", "يبكي"]}, {"ar": "وجّه المركب باتجاه إنجلترا، والوقت عم يركض.", "en": "He steered the ship toward England, with time running out.", "src": "book-atw80-ch35", "title": "Chapter 35 — Burning the Ship", "hi": ["عم", "يركض"]}, {"ar": "الكل حبس نفسه من الخوف وهو عم يعبر.", "en": "Everyone held their breath in fear as it crossed.", "src": "book-atw80-ch30", "title": "Chapter 30 — The Broken Bridge", "hi": ["عم", "يعبر"]}]}, {"id": "future", "title": "The future — رح", "sub": "رح أروح — “I’m going to go”", "body": ["For the future, put <b>رح</b> (ra7) — or its short form <b>حـ</b> stuck to the verb — before the <i>bare</i> present (no بـ): <b>رح أروح</b> / <b>حروح</b> “I’ll go”, <b>رح آكل</b> “I’ll eat”. Think of رح as “gonna”."], "examples": [{"ar": "الموظفة قالت إنه المكتبة رح تسكر.", "en": "The employee said the library was about to close.", "src": "story-int-30", "title": "A Day at the Library", "hi": ["رح", "تسكر"]}, {"ar": "مسكت إيده وقلتله ما تخاف، رح نلاقيها.", "en": "I held his hand and told him not to be scared, we'd find her.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["رح", "نلاقيها"]}, {"ar": "قالت: رح أوحشكم كتير.", "en": "She said: I'll miss you all a lot.", "src": "story-adv-12", "title": "The Wedding Night", "hi": ["رح", "أوحشكم"]}, {"ar": "قال: قلتلك رح نضل أصحاب للأبد.", "en": "He said: I told you we'd stay friends forever.", "src": "story-adv-10", "title": "The Promise", "hi": ["رح", "نضل"]}]}, {"id": "negation", "title": "Saying no — ما and مش", "sub": "ما بحب · مش هون", "body": ["Two tools. To negate a <b>verb</b>, put <b>ما</b> (ma) in front: <b>ما بحب</b> “I don’t like”, <b>ما رحت</b> “I didn’t go.” In casual speech you’ll often hear a <b>ـش</b> tacked on the end too: <b>ما بحبش</b>.", "To negate <b>everything else</b> — a noun, an adjective, a place — use <b>مش</b> (mish): <b>مش هون</b> “not here”, <b>مش كبير</b> “not big”, <b>مش أنا</b> “not me.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "بالأول، ما كنت متحمس كتير.", "en": "At first, I wasn't very excited.", "src": "story-int-20", "title": "The Book I Read", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "فتشت بجيبي وبالشنطة بس ما لقيته.", "en": "I searched my pocket and my bag but didn't find it.", "src": "story-int-03", "title": "The Lost Key", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "بالأول ما كنت بعرف وين الأشيا.", "en": "At first I didn't know where things were.", "src": "story-int-02", "title": "First Day at Work", "hi": ["ما"]}, {"ar": "الأكل كان بيتهني والحكي ما وقف.", "en": "The food was delicious and the talking didn't stop.", "src": "story-int-21", "title": "A Lunch Invitation", "hi": ["ما"]}]}, {"id": "baddi", "title": "Wanting — بدّي", "sub": "بدّي أروح — “I want to go”", "body": ["“Want” isn’t a normal verb — it’s the little word <b>بدّ</b> plus an ending for who wants: <b>بدّي</b> “I want”, <b>بدّك</b> “you want”, <b>بدّه</b> “he wants.” Follow it with a bare present verb for “want to …”: <b>بدّي أروح</b> “I want to go”, <b>بدّك تاكل؟</b> “do you want to eat?”"], "tables": [{"title": "بدّ + ending = want", "rows": [["بدّي", "baddi", "I want"], ["بدّك", "baddak", "you want (m)"], ["بدّك", "baddik", "you want (f)"], ["بدّه", "baddo", "he wants"], ["بدّها", "baddha", "she wants"], ["بدّنا", "baddna", "we want"], ["بدّكم", "baddkom", "you want (pl)"], ["بدّهم", "baddhom", "they want"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "اليوم بدي أساعد إمي بالطبخ.", "en": "Today I want to help my mom cook.", "src": "story-beg-18", "title": "Cooking with Mom", "hi": ["بدي"]}, {"ar": "بدي أشتري خبز وحليب وبيض.", "en": "I want to buy bread, milk, and eggs.", "src": "story-beg-29", "title": "An Errand", "hi": ["بدي"]}, {"ar": "صحينا بكير لأنه القطاف بدو وقت.", "en": "We woke up early because picking takes time.", "src": "story-int-24", "title": "The Trip to the Orchard", "hi": ["بدو"]}, {"ar": "كل واحد كان بدو الأرض الأحسن.", "en": "Each one wanted the better land.", "src": "story-adv-06", "title": "The Two Brothers", "hi": ["بدو"]}]}, {"id": "indi", "title": "Having — عندي", "sub": "عندي وقت — “I have time”", "body": ["Same idea for “have”: there’s no verb, just <b>عند</b> (“at / by”) plus the ending — literally “at-me / at-you”: <b>عندي</b> “I have”, <b>عندك</b> “you have”, <b>عنده</b> “he has.” <b>عندي وقت</b> “I have time”, <b>عندك سيارة؟</b> “do you have a car?”"], "tables": [{"title": "عند + ending = have", "rows": [["عندي", "3indi", "I have"], ["عندك", "3indak", "you have (m)"], ["عندك", "3indik", "you have (f)"], ["عنده", "3indo", "he has"], ["عندها", "3indha", "she has"], ["عندنا", "3indna", "we have"], ["عندكم", "3indkom", "you have (pl)"], ["عندهم", "3indhom", "they have"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "جارنا عنده كلب اسمه لولو.", "en": "Our neighbor has a dog named Lulu.", "src": "story-beg-26", "title": "The Dog", "hi": ["عنده"]}, {"ar": "عندي أخ وأخت، وهمة أصغر مني.", "en": "I have a brother and a sister, and they're younger than me.", "src": "story-beg-02", "title": "My Family", "hi": ["عندي"]}]}, {"id": "possessive", "title": "Mine, yours, his — endings on nouns", "sub": "بيتي، بيتك، بيته", "body": ["To say “my / your / his …”, you don’t use a separate word — you stick an ending on the noun, the same endings as بدّي and عندي: <b>بيت</b> “house” → <b>بيتي</b> “my house”, <b>بيتك</b> “your house”, <b>بيته</b> “his house”, <b>بيتنا</b> “our house.”"], "tables": [{"title": "بيت (house) + ending", "rows": [["بيتي", "beeti", "my house"], ["بيتك", "beetak", "your house (m)"], ["بيتك", "beetik", "your house (f)"], ["بيته", "beeto", "his house"], ["بيتها", "beetha", "her house"], ["بيتنا", "beetna", "our house"], ["بيتكم", "beetkom", "your house (pl)"], ["بيتهم", "beethom", "their house"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["بيتنا"]}, {"ar": "أم أحمد بتسكن جنبنا.", "en": "Um Ahmad lives next to us.", "src": "story-beg-12", "title": "The Neighbors", "hi": ["جنبنا"]}, {"ar": "عندي كتاب حلو بأوضتي.", "en": "I have a nice book in my room.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["عندي"]}, {"ar": "بسكر عيوني وبنام مبسوط.", "en": "I close my eyes and sleep happy.", "src": "story-beg-30", "title": "The Night", "hi": ["عيوني"]}]}, {"id": "questions", "title": "Asking things", "sub": "شو، وين، ليش…", "body": ["The question words. Word order stays the same as a statement — you just drop the question word in, usually at the front: <b>وين رايح؟</b> “where are you going?”, <b>شو بدّك؟</b> “what do you want?”"], "tables": [{"title": "Question words", "rows": [["شو", "shu", "what"], ["مين", "miin", "who"], ["وين", "ween", "where"], ["إيمتى", "eemta", "when"], ["ليش", "leesh", "why"], ["كيف", "kiif", "how"], ["قدّيش", "2addeesh", "how much"], ["كم", "kam", "how many"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "قربت منه وسألته وين إمه.", "en": "I got close to him and asked where his mom was.", "src": "story-int-16", "title": "The Boy Who Got Lost", "hi": ["وين"]}, {"ar": "بالأول ما كنت بعرف وين الأشيا.", "en": "At first I didn't know where things were.", "src": "story-int-02", "title": "First Day at Work", "hi": ["وين"]}, {"ar": "خفت كتير وما عرفت شو أقول.", "en": "I got very scared and didn't know what to say.", "src": "story-int-27", "title": "The Broken Cup", "hi": ["شو"]}, {"ar": "سأل شو المشكلة وحاول يساعد أبوي.", "en": "He asked what the problem was and tried to help my dad.", "src": "story-int-11", "title": "The Car That Broke Down", "hi": ["شو"]}]}, {"id": "demonstratives", "title": "This and these", "sub": "هاد، هاي، هدول", "body": ["“This” changes with gender, “these” doesn’t: <b>هاد</b> (haad) for a masculine thing, <b>هاي</b> (hayy) for a feminine one, <b>هدول</b> (hadool) for plural. <b>هاد الولد</b> “this boy”, <b>هاي البنت</b> “this girl”, <b>هدول الناس</b> “these people.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "رجعت عالضيعة، بس هالمرة كمعلمة.", "en": "She returned to the village, but this time as a teacher.", "src": "story-adv-07", "title": "The Girl Who Loved to Learn", "hi": ["هالمرة"]}, {"ar": "العيلة كلها زعلت من هالوضع.", "en": "The whole family was saddened by the situation.", "src": "story-adv-06", "title": "The Two Brothers", "hi": ["هالوضع"]}, {"ar": "قال: هالأرض عمرتها بعرق جبيني.", "en": "He said: I built up this land with the sweat of my brow.", "src": "story-adv-15", "title": "The Last Harvest", "hi": ["هالأرض"]}, {"ar": "قال: هاي مكافأة على أمانتك.", "en": "He said: this is a reward for your integrity.", "src": "story-adv-22", "title": "The Debt", "hi": ["هاي"]}]}, {"id": "relative", "title": "The one that… — اللي", "sub": "الولد اللي بيلعب", "body": ["One little word does the job of “who / which / that”: <b>اللي</b> (illi), for people and things alike. <b>الولد اللي بيلعب</b> “the boy who’s playing”, <b>الأكل اللي طبخته</b> “the food that I cooked.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "الولاد كانوا يلعبوا ويجمعوا الزيتون اللي وقع.", "en": "The kids were playing and gathering the olives that fell.", "src": "story-int-24", "title": "The Trip to the Orchard", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "ضحك وقال: البحر بيحترم اللي بيحترمه.", "en": "He laughed and said: the sea respects those who respect it.", "src": "story-adv-13", "title": "The Fisherman", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "قال: الله يجازي اللي عمل هالخير.", "en": "He said: may God reward whoever did this good deed.", "src": "story-adv-14", "title": "The Poor Neighbor", "hi": ["اللي"]}, {"ar": "أبوي قال: الخير اللي بتعمله، بيرجعلك.", "en": "My dad said: the good you do comes back to you.", "src": "story-adv-03", "title": "The Stranger Guest", "hi": ["اللي"]}]}, {"id": "kaan", "title": "Was and were — كان", "sub": "كان تعبان — “he was tired”", "body": ["Remember there’s no “is” in the present — but there <i>is</i> a past “was / were”: <b>كان</b> (kaan). <b>كان تعبان</b> “he was tired”, <b>كنت هون</b> “I was here”, <b>كانوا مبسوطين</b> “they were happy.” Put كان before a present verb and you get “used to / was …-ing”: <b>كنت بلعب</b> “I used to play.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "كان خايف وجناحه موجوع.", "en": "It was scared and its wing was hurt.", "src": "story-int-28", "title": "The Bird", "hi": ["كان"]}, {"ar": "المكتبة كانت كبيرة وهادية.", "en": "The library was big and quiet.", "src": "story-int-30", "title": "A Day at the Library", "hi": ["كانت"]}, {"ar": "كان يوم ما بننساه.", "en": "It was a day we won't forget.", "src": "story-int-01", "title": "A Trip to the Sea", "hi": ["كان"]}, {"ar": "جدتي كانت أسعد وحدة بالعرس.", "en": "My grandmother was the happiest one at the wedding.", "src": "story-int-04", "title": "My Cousin's Wedding", "hi": ["كانت"]}]}, {"id": "past", "title": "Telling what happened — the past", "sub": "رحت، أكلت، شفت", "body": ["Stories run on the past tense, and it’s built by changing the <i>end</i> of the verb instead of the front: <b>راح</b> “he went”, <b>رحت</b> “I went”, <b>رحنا</b> “we went.” No بـ here — the past never takes it.", "The full set of endings, for any verb and any pattern, is in the <b>Verbs</b> section — open a verb and look at the “Past” column."], "examples": [{"ar": "الشوارع صارت مليانة مي.", "en": "The streets got full of water.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["صارت"]}, {"ar": "اليوم إمي طبخت ملوخية ورز.", "en": "Today my mom cooked mulukhiyah and rice.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["طبخت"]}, {"ar": "اليوم نزلت شتا من الصبح.", "en": "Today it rained from the morning.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["نزلت"]}, {"ar": "قعدت بالبيت وشربت شاي سخن.", "en": "I stayed home and drank hot tea.", "src": "story-beg-16", "title": "The Rain", "hi": ["قعدت"]}]}, {"id": "comparative", "title": "Bigger, better — comparatives", "sub": "أكبر من · أحسن", "body": ["To compare, most adjectives take an <b>أ-</b> shape: <b>كبير</b> “big” → <b>أكبر</b> “bigger”, <b>حلو</b> → <b>أحلى</b> “sweeter/nicer”, <b>كتير</b> → <b>أكتر</b> “more.” Add <b>من</b> for “than”: <b>أكبر من بيتنا</b> “bigger than our house.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "المسا بالبيت أحلى وقت.", "en": "The evening at home is the nicest time.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "هو أحسن صاحب عندي.", "en": "He's my best friend.", "src": "story-beg-07", "title": "My Friend", "hi": ["أحسن"]}, {"ar": "الخبز السخن أحلى شي.", "en": "Hot bread is the nicest thing.", "src": "story-beg-19", "title": "The Bakery", "hi": ["أحلى"]}, {"ar": "الشاي بالنعنع أحلى شي بالمسا.", "en": "Mint tea is the nicest thing in the evening.", "src": "story-beg-09", "title": "Tea", "hi": ["أحلى"]}]}, {"id": "fi", "title": "“There is / there are” — في (and “in” is بـ)", "sub": "في أكل · ما في وقت · بالبيت", "body": ["This one confuses English speakers because <b>في</b> <i>looks</i> like it should mean “in” — and in Modern Standard Arabic it does. But in spoken Palestinian, <b>في</b> (fi) usually means <b>“there is / there are”</b>: <b>في أكل</b> “there’s food”, <b>في ناس كتير</b> “there are a lot of people.” The negative is <b>ما في</b>: <b>ما في وقت</b> “there’s no time.”", "So how do you say <b>“in / at”</b>? Palestinian sticks <b>بـ</b> (b-) onto the noun instead: <b>بالبيت</b> “in the house”, <b>بالسيارة</b> “in the car”, <b>بالشغل</b> “at work.” Keep the two apart — <b>في</b> = there is, <b>بـ</b> = in — and في stops feeling random."], "examples": [{"ar": "فيه قصص وصور كتير.", "en": "It has stories and many pictures.", "src": "story-beg-15", "title": "A Book", "hi": ["فيه"]}, {"ar": "فيها شجر زيتون وليمون.", "en": "It has olive and lemon trees.", "src": "story-beg-10", "title": "The Garden", "hi": ["فيها"]}, {"ar": "فيها سرير وطاولة وكرسي.", "en": "It has a bed, a table, and a chair.", "src": "story-beg-17", "title": "My Room", "hi": ["فيها"]}, {"ar": "عالحيط في صورة للبحر.", "en": "On the wall there is a picture of the sea.", "src": "story-beg-17", "title": "My Room", "hi": ["في"]}]}, {"id": "idafa", "title": "No word for “of” — just stack the nouns", "sub": "باب البيت = the door of the house", "body": ["English links nouns with “of” or “’s” (“the door <i>of</i> the house”, “the house<i>’s</i> door”). Arabic just sets the two nouns next to each other, the owned thing first, with <b>no</b> word for “of”: <b>باب البيت</b> “the door of the house”, <b>بيت الرجل</b> “the man’s house”, <b>اسم البنت</b> “the girl’s name.”", "Watch the “the”: only the <i>second</i> noun takes الـ — <b>باب البيت</b>, literally “door the-house” (never الباب البيت). For a looser “belonging to,” spoken Palestinian also has <b>تبع</b>: <b>الكتاب تبع أحمد</b> “Ahmad’s book.”"], "examples": [{"ar": "بحب درس العربي والرسم.", "en": "I like Arabic class and drawing.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["درس", "العربي"]}, {"ar": "بحب صوت المطر عالشباك.", "en": "I like the sound of rain on the window.", "src": "story-beg-08", "title": "The Weather Today", "hi": ["صوت", "المطر"]}, {"ar": "يوم الجمعة بروح عالسوق.", "en": "On Friday I go to the market.", "src": "story-beg-06", "title": "To the Market", "hi": ["يوم", "الجمعة"]}, {"ar": "بحب ليالي الصيف الطويلة.", "en": "I like the long summer nights.", "src": "story-beg-28", "title": "Summer", "hi": ["ليالي", "الصيف"]}]}, {"id": "gender", "title": "Masculine & feminine", "sub": "ولد كبير · بنت كبيرة", "body": ["Arabic sorts every noun as masculine or feminine, and the words around it must match — something English never does. The usual sign of feminine is a <b>ة</b> ending: <b>بنت</b> “girl”, <b>سيارة</b> “car”, <b>قهوة</b> “coffee” are feminine; most other nouns are masculine.", "Adjectives and verbs agree with it. Masculine <b>ولد كبير</b> “a big boy” → feminine <b>بنت كبيرة</b> “a big girl” (add ة). <b>هو راح</b> “he went” → <b>هي راحت</b> “she went.” Learn each noun’s gender together with the word — there’s no shortcut, but the ة gives most of them away."], "examples": [{"ar": "بروح عالمدرسة كل الصبح.", "en": "I go to school every morning.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["عالمدرسة"]}, {"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["قريبة"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["الطاولة"]}, {"ar": "بحب أشتري خضرة طازة.", "en": "I like to buy fresh vegetables.", "src": "story-beg-25", "title": "The Vegetables", "hi": ["خضرة"]}]}, {"id": "wadi-ara", "title": "The Wadi Ara accent", "sub": "وادي عارة — how the Triangle villages sound", "body": ["This app teaches <b>urban Palestinian</b> — the city speech of Jerusalem, Ramallah and Haifa that works everywhere, Wadi Ara included. But the home accent of Wadi Ara — Umm il-Faḥm, ʿArʿara, Kufur Qariʿ, Bāqa — is <b>central rural Palestinian</b> (“fallāḥi”), the village dialect of the Triangle and the central hills. Same grammar, same words; what changes is a handful of sounds, and they change <i>systematically</i> — learn four correspondences and you can convert anything you know on the fly.", "First: <b>ق is said k</b>. وقت “time” is city <i>wa2it</i>, village <i>wakit</i>; قهوة is <i>2ahwe</i> in town, <i>kahwe</i> in the village. And <b>ك is said ch</b> (č, as in “church”): كيف <i>kiif</i> → <i>chiif</i>, حكى&lrm; <i>7aka</i> → <i>7acha</i>; “your (f.)” <b>ـِك</b> becomes <i>-ich</i>. The two shifts work as a pair — ك moved to <i>ch</i> and freed <i>k</i> for ق — so in Wadi Ara <i>kalb</i> is قلب “heart”, while “dog” (كلب) is <i>chalb</i>. No confusion, ever.", "The <b>interdentals survive</b> in the village where the city dropped them: ث stays <i>th</i> (ثاني city <i>taani</i>, village <i>thaani</i>) and ذ stays <i>dh</i> (هاد ~ <i>haadh</i>). Traditional village speech also keeps the <b>feminine plurals</b> the city lost — إنتن <i>intin</i> “you (f. pl.)”, هنّ <i>hinne</i> “they (f.)”, verbs ending in <i>ـِن</i> — today mostly from older speakers. Younger Wadi Ara speakers mix city forms freely, but the <i>ch</i> stays a proud local badge.", "Everything in this app — spelling, tables, audio — stays in the urban koine, because that is what the recorded voice speaks and what every Palestinian understands. The word cards show the Wadi Ara form automatically whenever it differs, and the examples below are corpus sentences containing words a Wadi Ara speaker says differently — try converting them as you read."], "tables": [{"title": "The four correspondences", "rows": [["ق", "city: 2 (glottal stop)", "Wadi Ara: k"], ["ك", "city: k", "Wadi Ara: ch (č, as in “church”)"], ["ث", "city: t", "Wadi Ara: th (as in “three”)"], ["ذ", "city: d", "Wadi Ara: dh (as in “this”)"]]}, {"title": "Same word, two accents — computed from the lexicon’s own templates", "rows": [["وَقِت", "wa2it → wakit", "time"], ["قَدِيم", "2adiim → kadiim", "old"], ["طَرِيق", "t.arii2 → t.ariik", "road"], ["حَكَى", "7aka → 7acha", "he spoke"], ["ثَانِي", "taani → thaani", "second, other"], ["كْثِير", "ktiir → chthiir", "a lot, many"]]}], "examples": [{"ar": "المدرسة قريبة من بيتنا.", "en": "The school is close to our house.", "src": "story-beg-14", "title": "School", "hi": ["قريبة"]}, {"ar": "كلنا بنقعد حول الطاولة.", "en": "We all sit around the table.", "src": "story-beg-13", "title": "Lunch", "hi": ["بنقعد"]}, {"ar": "بالمسا، بنقعد كلنا بالصالون.", "en": "In the evening, we all sit in the living room.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["بنقعد"]}, {"ar": "إمي بتخيط وأنا بقرا.", "en": "My mom sews and I read.", "src": "story-beg-22", "title": "The Evening", "hi": ["بقرا"]}]}]};
